INTRO The goal here is learning, not testing. Take your time and think about the questions. You can go through as many times as necessary to get the answers right, but you're not learning unless you're learning. 

MC Assume α=0.05. A frequentist test is valid if – under the counter-factual that the null hypothesis is true – it ______ the null hypothesis at least _____ of the time.

accepts; 0.05

accepts; 0.95

fails to reject; 0.05

* fails to reject; 0.95

FF The baseline is: if there's nothing to see, we will hallucinate (reject the null) less than α of the time.

MC Which of these is the _best_ interpretation of a non-significant result in our vitamin A comparison between two groups of children, according to Dushoff?:
______ helping or hurting children (as measured by the metric we are studying)

We have no evidence whether vitamin A is 

* We are not confident whether

We have some evidence that vitamin A is _not_

We are confident that vitamin A is _not_

FF Non-significant results in simple tests mean we're not clear about the sign (in this case the positive or negative effect of the treatment on the outcome)

MC Tests that are “asymptotically valid” conform to the frequentist perspective ______, and should be used ______.

absolutely; cautiously

* only when sample size is large; cautiously

absolutely; whenever possible

only when sample size is large; whenever possible

FF Just a definition from the notes, plus common sense (it's not always valid, so be cautious)

MC The best evidence that an effect is unimportant (if you don't have a confidence interval) would be ______ estimate of effect size and ______ P value

any; a low 

any; a high 

* a small; a low 

a small; a high 

FF It's hard without CIs, but the point here is that -- for a given effect size -- a smaller P value means a smaller CI, so things will stay closer to zero.
